-- TAIL-POSITION CALL/DEFINE ARITY CONFORMANCE (#1101 S0).
--
-- The regression guard that replaces `test/must_fail_fixtures/1101-native-call-
-- drops-arg-caf-closure/`, drained and deleted when #1101 was fixed.
--
-- THE DEFECT.  `emitFnBody`'s tail-position known-fn arm (llvm_emit.mdk) emitted an
-- UNCONDITIONAL direct `[musttail] call @mdk_<fn>(<every flattened argument>)` — it
-- never consulted the callee's arity at all, unlike `emitApp`'s known-fn arm, which
-- routes through the arity-disciplined `emitKnownFnSat`.  LLVM's opaque-pointer call
-- model does not cross-check a direct call's argument count against the callee's
-- `define`, so an over-applied tail call was legal-but-wrong IR: it linked, it ran,
-- the surplus arguments landed in unread registers, and the RETURNED CLOSURE WAS
-- NEVER INVOKED.  check/run/build all reported success; only the executed binary
-- was wrong, silently (#1101's own repro printed nothing at exit 0 where the
-- interpreter printed `CALLED`).
--
-- WHY THIS FIXTURE IS WIDER THAN #1101'S PROSE.  The issue (and its must-fail
-- claim.txt) asserted the repro needed BOTH a top-level lazy nullary (CAF) AND a
-- separate top-level caller.  The CAF half is FALSE — measured during the
-- S-caf-closure-apply spike.  The TRUE minimal isolation is two conditions, neither
-- of them the CAF:
--   (1) a NON-SATURATED application of a known top-level function, and
--   (2) that application sitting in TAIL position of a non-`main` top-level binding
--       (i.e. reached by `emitFnBody`, never by `emitApp`).
-- `MyBox`/`Ref`/the lazy global are incidental.  A fixture built only from #1101's
-- CAF-flavoured shape would under-pin the fix, so all four cells live here in one
-- program behind ONE golden line: a fix that re-breaks any single cell reds this
-- gate rather than passing quietly.
--
-- The expected stdout in `tail_over_application_arity_conformance.build.golden` is
-- HAND-DERIVED from the language semantics, written down before either engine was
-- consulted ([WT-GOLDEN-ENSHRINES]) — the interpreter agreed on every cell both
-- before AND during the defect, so a capture would have proven nothing.
--
-- The derivation, straight off the source:
--   plainTail = 15   `mk 10` is `x => 10 + x`; applied to 5.
--   cafTail   = 42   the boxed closure is `n => n * 3`; applied to 14.
--   pap       = 6    `add3 1` is a 2-of-3 PAP; applied to 2 and 3 -> 1 + 2 + 3.
--   inline    = 107  `(mk 100) 7` -> 100 + 7, in `main`'s own body.
--   loop      = 15   0+5+4+3+2+1.

-- CELL 1: OVER-APPLICATION in tail position of a non-`main` top-level fn, with no
-- CAF, no `Ref` and no `data` declaration anywhere in sight.  This is the widened
-- shape the spike measured; it is the cell #1101's own fixture never contained.
-- Pre-fix this emitted `call @mdk_mk(i64 %n, i64 %x)` against `define @mdk_mk(i64
-- %arg0)` and returned the un-invoked closure word as if it were the Int result.
mk : Int -> Int -> Int
mk n = x => n + x

plainTail : Unit -> Int
plainTail u = (mk 10) 5

-- CELL 2: #1101's OWN shape, kept verbatim in spirit — the closure reached through
-- a top-level lazy nullary (CAF) holding a `Ref` inside a constructor.  Same emitter
-- arm, same skew; retained so the originally-pinned program stays covered even
-- though its CAF is not what made it fail.
data MyBox a = MyBox (Ref a)

getBox : MyBox a -> a
getBox (MyBox r) = r.value

box : MyBox (Int -> Int)
box = MyBox (Ref (n => n * 3))

cafTail : Unit -> Int
cafTail u = (getBox box) 14

-- CELL 3: the OTHER side of the same missing check — UNDER-application in tail
-- position.  The new guard is a saturation test, not an over-application test, so a
-- fix that special-cased only `nargs > arity` would leave this emitting
-- `call @mdk_add3(i64 1)` against a three-param define, reading two garbage words.
-- Correct lowering builds a partial-application closure (`emitPapClosure`).
add3 : Int -> Int -> Int -> Int
add3 a b c = a + b + c

mkAdder : Unit -> (Int -> Int -> Int)
mkAdder u = add3 1

-- CELL 4: the arm that must NOT move — a SATURATED self-recursive tail call, which
-- is exactly the common case the guard is designed to leave byte-identical
-- (`musttail call` + `ret`).  If the guard ever mis-measures the saturated case,
-- this loop stops being a tail call and the emitted IR churns.
sumTo : Int -> Int -> Int
sumTo acc n = if n == 0 then acc else sumTo (acc + n) (n - 1)

loop : Unit -> Int
loop u = sumTo 0 5

-- `inline=` is the control cell: the SAME over-application, but in `main`'s own
-- body, which reaches `emitApp` instead of `emitFnBody` and was correct all along.
main =
  println
    "plainTail=\{debug (plainTail ())} cafTail=\{debug (cafTail ())} pap=\{debug (mkAdder () 2 3)} inline=\{debug ((mk 100) 7)} loop=\{debug (loop ())}"
